# `rows.map(row => row.v)`, copied. Where nothing is known of what the arrow gives, or the row's layout is a guess, the closed copy of the run stored by TryPutIndex, which may turn the list (int32 into fractions or any value) and
# lengthen it. What a loop does is one answer for both its copies, so the typed copy could not ask the kind of its own list in front of the turns: it asked the shape and the bound at every store, and one odd row sent the rest of
# the call through mapFrom at every call.
# In a copied run that store now stays TryPutByValDirect without a kind: it takes the list as it finds it or refuses, and writes elements alone (guessed, unknown). The typed copy asks once in front whether the list takes numbers
# by its kind of storage and stores without a test that can refuse; what may be NaN is asked in the turn (proven, guessed). The place remembers only where that store was turned so (guessed); where every value is proven a number
# nothing is remembered (proven). The TryPutIndex that is left stands behind the run, where a refused store is made.
# A list without a kind was born for int32: the first other value was refused. Where a copied run's store lays the memory on such a birth, the first lists of the place take any value and the first review reads what the list
# holds for the narrowest kind (unknown: takesAnyValueAtFirst); a refused store makes the next birth a reviewed one. `new Array(n)` is not touched.
# -- source: a-copied-run-stores-into-its-list-as-it-finds-it.cjs
